FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] docs/GRAMMAR.md:325 — `try_statement` wraps the catch clause in `{ 'catch' ... }` (zero-or-more), allowing multiple catches — contradicts SPEC.md:915 (`A try has one catch. There is no second catch`) and src/parser.rs:1638 which parses a single `catch` — concrete fix: change the repetition to an optional `[ 'catch' ... ]`.
2. [MAJOR] docs/GRAMMAR.md:327 — production allows `catch <any-identifier> [ <any-identifier> ] [ 'of' failure_kind ... ]`, but src/parser.rs:2039 requires the word `error` (`is_word_ahead(0, "error")`) before `of` is even considered (src/parser.rs:1667), so `catch problem of Runtime` is grammar-valid yet parses as binder `problem` plus orphan statements — concrete fix: restrict the grammar to `catch error [of ...]` for the typed form.
3. [MAJOR] tests/typed_catch_test.rs:127 — `agree` compares failures by `label() == label() && message() == message()` only, ignoring `span()`/line — the phase's own claim (src/failure.rs:19) is that `line` is the same on both engines, so a bytecode `line_span` regression on an uncaught failure would still pass — concrete fix: also compare `error.span()` line (or the reported `line` field) in `agree`/`failure`.
REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
